Related: #69302 and #69310 fixed the initial `0 -> N` provider load, but a subsequent stale-anchor correction can still undo the initial end scroll.

### Describe the bug

`Virtualize<TItem>` with an `ItemsProvider` and `AnchorMode="VirtualizeAnchorMode.End"` can briefly scroll to the correct bottom and then immediately restore a stale pre-append anchor. It remains at that partial position until a later `refreshObservedElements` call pins it to the bottom again.

This is visible in live log/chat scenarios as roughly a one-second pause at an older item before jumping to the latest item.

The problem occurs in `refreshObservedElements` when end following and `pendingScrollCorrection` are both active:

```ts
if (bottomTracking.following
    || (anchorModeIs.end && (bottomTracking.wasAtBottomLastRender || bottomTracking.reached))) {
  flushPendingStyleMutations();
  scrollElement.scrollTop = scrollElement.scrollHeight;
  // ...
}

if (pendingScrollCorrection) {
  // ...
  scrollElement.scrollTop += delta;
}
```

The first assignment reaches the end. The correction immediately afterward uses the previous anchor and moves the viewport back into the list.

### Expected behavior

When `AnchorMode.End` is following the tail, spacer drift correction from the previous window should not undo the end-anchor assignment. The viewport should remain at the bottom during the first render containing the appended items.

### Actual behavior

Playwright instrumentation of a real `ItemsProvider` transition from one item to 386 items recorded the following. The viewport height was 441 px and the final scroll height was 8,512 px:

| Time | Operation | Result |
|------|-----------|--------|
| 317 ms | Leading spacer becomes 3,500 px | Initial partial scroll to `scrollTop=3500` |
| 323 ms | `refreshObservedElements` assigns `scrollTop=scrollHeight` | Correctly reaches `scrollTop=8071` |
| 323 ms | The same `refreshObservedElements` call applies pending drift correction | Immediately returns to `scrollTop=3500` |
| 1,634 ms | A later `refreshObservedElements` assigns `scrollTop=scrollHeight` | Finally reaches and remains at `scrollTop=8071` |

At the time of the conflicting assignments:

- Rendered rows: 228
- First rendered item: 159
- Last rendered item: 386
- Leading spacer: 3,500 px
- Trailing spacer: 0 px

Because the trailing spacer is already zero, the conditional at `spacerAfter.offsetHeight > 0` does not start bottom convergence. The pending correction therefore wins until a later observer refresh happens for another reason.

### Minimal reproduction shape

The issue can be reproduced with an end-anchored provider that initially contains one row and asynchronously appends a large batch:

```razor
<div style="height: 441px; overflow-y: auto">
    <Virtualize @ref="_virtualize"
                ItemsProvider="LoadItemsAsync"
                ItemSize="20"
                OverscanCount="100"
                AnchorMode="VirtualizeAnchorMode.End">
        <div style="height: 22px">@context</div>
    </Virtualize>
</div>

@code {
    private readonly List<int> _items = [1];
    private Virtualize<int>? _virtualize;
    private bool _appended;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender && !_appended)
        {
            _appended = true;
            await Task.Delay(100);
            _items.AddRange(Enumerable.Range(2, 385));
            await _virtualize!.RefreshDataAsync();
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private ValueTask<ItemsProviderResult<int>> LoadItemsAsync(ItemsProviderRequest request)
    {
        var items = _items.Skip(request.StartIndex).Take(request.Count);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(new ItemsProviderResult<int>(items, _items.Count));
    }
}
```

Instrumenting the scroll container's `scrollTop` setter shows both conflicting assignments originate from `refreshObservedElements` in `blazor.web.js`.

### Possible fix

Skip the pending spacer drift correction when the same refresh is actively following/pinning the end, or clear/rebase the pending correction after the end assignment. The end anchor is the intended viewport state in this path; restoring the previous anchor conflicts with it.

### Version

- .NET SDK: `11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105`
- ASP.NET Core browser assets: `11.0.0-rc.2.26504.105`
- OS: Windows 11, win-x64
- Browser: Chromium

